WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.584
Google DeepMind: Demis Hassabis and the lab’s own paper.

2
00:00:03.864 --> 00:00:12.653
Google DeepMind’s public position is more hedged and has two sources worth separating: what the lab wrote, and what its leader said on stage.

3
00:00:12.933 --> 00:00:14.085
The lab’s paper.

4
00:00:14.365 --> 00:00:23.347
In April 2025, Google DeepMind published “An Approach to Technical AGI Safety and Security” (Shah and colleagues).

5
00:00:23.627 --> 00:00:34.955
It stated that the authors found it plausible that powerful AI systems could be developed by 2030, and used that as a planning assumption for safety work rather than a prediction.

6
00:00:35.235 --> 00:00:44.088
Treating a date as a planning assumption is a useful model for your own team: you can prepare for a possibility without betting the company that it will happen.

7
00:00:44.368 --> 00:00:45.691
Hassabis on stage.

8
00:00:45.971 --> 00:01:05.491
At Davos in January 2026, as reported by Fortune, Hassabis put the chance of human-level AGI within the decade at about 50%, gave a range of five to ten years, and said one or two more breakthroughs might be needed, which is a different view from “scaling the current recipe will get there.

9
00:01:05.771 --> 00:01:15.904
” He has given other estimates in later 2026 interviews, and secondhand reports of them vary in wording; read a primary transcript before quoting any of them.
